$ enola help
exit: 0
--- stdout
--- stderr
enola — MCP server for architectural snapshots

Give your AI agent a map of the codebase before it starts exploring.

USAGE
  enola [flags] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola dashboard [--open] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola cluster init [--dry-run] [dir]
  enola baseline <pin|show|clear> [repo_path|config_path]
  enola check [flags] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola constraints <lint|mine|ledger> [repo_path|config_path]
  enola plan [flags] [path...] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola coverage [flags] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola impact [flags] <target> [repo_path|config_path]
  enola flows [flags] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola doctor [repo_path]
  enola log [flags] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola show [<revision>] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola diff <revA>..<revB> [repo_path|config_path]
  enola blame [flags] <pattern> [repo_path|config_path]
  enola gc [flags] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola history <push|pull|verify|gc> [store_dir] [repo_path|config_path]
  enola install [--hooks] [--global] [--targets=…] [repo_path]
  enola upgrade

COMMANDS
  dashboard           Explore the latest snapshot in a read-only local web dashboard,
                      without starting an MCP server. Add --open to launch the browser.
                      Stays attached to the terminal; stop with Ctrl-C.
  cluster             Write a cluster config for a folder that holds several git
                      repositories. "init" lists every immediate subfolder that is a
                      repository in cluster.yaml, refusing to overwrite; pass that file
                      to --generate to index them as one linked graph.
  install             Write enola's instructions into the files your coding
                      agents read (Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot, Codex, Pi, opencode,
                      AGENTS.md). Previews every change and asks before writing.
                        --hooks   also run the loop automatically, instead of leaving
                                  the agent to remember. In Claude Code, Codex and Pi:
                                  pin a baseline at session start and report the
                                  architectural delta at the end, but only if the
                                  change introduced a regression. In opencode, which
                                  has no hooks of that shape: a plugin that points the
                                  session's first searches at the index instead.
                                  Opt-in, because both run code. Pi always gets its
                                  extension, which serves the tools; --hooks only adds
                                  the session hooks to it.
                        --targets a comma-separated subset, for narrowing a run. The
                                  default is every target, and a target whose agent is
                                  not installed skips itself and says so.
                        --global  configure this user rather than this repository.
                        --dry-run show what would change and write nothing.
  uninstall           Remove everything "install" wrote, leaving the rest of each
                      file byte-for-byte as it was.
  baseline            Manage the diff baseline — the "before" your changes are graded
                      against. "pin" snapshots the repository and freezes it (no separate
                      --generate needed), "show" reports what the current baseline
                      describes, "clear" removes it. The baseline is stored per-repository,
                      in that repo's output dir, so several repos each keep their own.
  check               Grade what a change did to the architecture against the pinned
                      baseline, and exit with a code CI can act on:
                        0 clean · 1 regression · 2 error · 3 declined (not comparable)
                      Read-only by default — nothing is written, and the baseline stays
                      put, so it can be run as often as you like. Run
                      "enola check --help" for the flags.
  constraints         Author the declared constraint vocabulary. "lint" parses each repo's
                      enola-intent.yaml (and any cluster-config intent override), reports
                      every validation problem with its file context, and resolves each
                      declared component against the current snapshot if one exists —
                      so a selector that matches nothing is caught while authoring, not
                      by a vacuously-passing rule. Exits 1 on validation problems.
                      "mine" searches the snapshot's fact store for near-invariants and
                      reports candidate rules with their evidence and named exceptions —
                      proposals for review, never self-adopting law.
                      "ledger" reports how much of the law you already declared is being
                      EXCUSED rather than obeyed — each rule's breaches beside the
                      suppressions and exemptions that signed them away, with every
                      excuse's owner and age. Evidence for judging a rule, never a gate.
  plan                The pre-edit contract: which declared constraints govern an
                      intended change (--paths, --symbols), its blast radius over the
                      current snapshot, and — for a --patch — the constraint verdicts
                      that WOULD appear, evaluated over a scratch copy BEFORE any edit
                      lands in the tree. Nothing is written; a report, never a gate.
                      Run "enola plan --help" for the flags.
  coverage            Report which cross-repo edges were resolved and which were not,
                      per service — telling a genuinely isolated service apart from one
                      whose outbound edges could not be followed. Needs two or more
                      repositories in one graph. A report, not a gate: always exits 0.
  endpoint            Report what changing an HTTP endpoint reaches: the controller
                      serving it, the models that controller touches, the models
                      associated with those, the tables behind them, and the callers,
                      including the frontend screen a calling route module implements.
                      Use impact when you have a symbol; use this when what you have
                      is a URL.
  impact              Report the blast radius of changing a symbol, type or module:
                      everything that transitively depends on it, by hop depth, with an
                      exact total. --list names the dependents, --json emits
                      the raw graph with each one's file and line. The impact_analysis
                      tool, without a session. Exits 2 when nothing was traversed
                      because the target matched nothing or too many nodes, so an empty
                      answer is never an unasked question.
                      Run "enola impact --help" for the flags.
  flows               Rank the ways into a program by what each reaches. An entry is a
                      served route's handler, a program's main, or a symbol a framework
                      invokes; its flow is every symbol the calls from it reach. --show
                      describes one flow with its stages, --through lists the flows that
                      reach a symbol, --json emits the report. A flow is a floor: each one
                      counts the calls whose target is no symbol in the graph.
                      Run "enola flows --help" for the flags.
  log                 EXPERIMENTAL. Show what this repository's architecture has done over
                      time — one line per recorded snapshot, with what changed since the
                      one before it. Read-only: it reports what was observed and never
                      snapshots to fill a gap. Every snapshot is recorded as a revision
                      (~450 bytes, outside the repo); set `history.enabled: false` to stop.
  show                EXPERIMENTAL. Show what ONE recorded revision did to the architecture
                      — "log" says a revision added twelve facts, this says which twelve.
                      Reconstructs the revision and its predecessor out of
                      the stored history and compares them, so a past change is described in the same words it
                      was described in at the time. A revision is a snapshot id or prefix, a
                      git commit, HEAD~N, @<seq>, a ref name, or `latest` (the default).
  diff                EXPERIMENTAL. Show the architecture delta between any two recorded
                      revisions — the question a week of work produces, where "show" answers
                      for a single one. Either side of the range may be empty, meaning the
                      oldest or newest recorded revision.
  blame               EXPERIMENTAL. Show when something entered the architecture and when
                      it left — "when did this module start importing that one?", which a
                      snapshot cannot answer however good it is, because it is a question
                      about the past. Matches a name, a path, or both ends of an edge
                      against the recorded facts; --findings searches findings instead,
                      and --first stops at the introduction.
  gc                  EXPERIMENTAL. Report what the architecture history holds — how many
                      revisions, how many can still be replayed, how much disk — and remove
                      what it no longer needs. With no flags it removes only garbage;
                      --thin-older-than and --prune-working discard things a reader could
                      still reach, so each has to be asked for.
  history             EXPERIMENTAL. Share the architecture history between machines through
                      a directory store — a git repository, a shared mount, an S3-synced
                      folder. Plain files, content-addressed, tamper-evident. "push" copies
                      local revisions in, "pull" imports what other machines pushed,
                      "verify" walks every chain and names gaps and tampering, "gc" applies
                      retention — printed first, deleted only with --apply, recorded in the
                      chain. Point it with history.shared_dir or the first argument.
  doctor              Report whether the session hooks are actually FIRING in this
                      repository, not merely configured. `install --hooks` can write a
                      configuration your agent silently ignores — it reports success
                      either way — so this asks the only question that settles it: when
                      did each hook last run, and what did it conclude? A report, not a
                      gate: always exits 0.
  providers           The fact providers this binary carries itself. `providers list`
                      names each and whether it is ready; `providers fetch rubydex`
                      downloads the pinned Rubydex engine library, verifies its published
                      digest, and caches it, after which a `providers:` entry named
                      rubydex with no command runs in-process. The only network access a
                      provider ever makes, and never at snapshot time.
  upgrade             Download and install the latest enola release, replacing the
                      running binary in place.

FLAGS
  --generate          Generate a snapshot and exit (do not start MCP server)
  --refresh           With --generate on a cluster config: re-read ONE configured repository
                      into the union the last full generate produced, replacing its slice,
                      and write the result to that repository and to the union's home. The
                      other repositories are not re-read. Needs a prior full --generate.
  --explain [path]    Print a human-readable repository statistics report and exit.
                      A directory is a repository; a file is a config, so a `repos:`
                      config reports over the whole cluster.
  --list              List the MCP tools this build can serve
  --status            Show MCP server status: uptime, tool usage and estimated value,
                      aggregated across every repo the server has served. While the server
                      is running this also prints the dashboard URL.
  --status --all      Show the per-repo breakdown instead (from ~/.enola/usage/)
  --no-dashboard      Do not start the localhost dashboard alongside the MCP server
  --no-cluster        With --generate on a folder that holds several git repositories:
                      index it as one repository instead of as a cluster
  --version           Print version information
  --version --json    Print the version and the extractor version as JSON, on stdout.
                      This is the release manifest: what a build is called, and what it
                      EXTRACTS LIKE. See UPDATES.
  --help, -h          Show this help message

CONFIG_PATH
  Path to the config file (default: mcp-arch.yaml). Set `repos:` in it to
  name a multi-repo cluster; entries resolve relative to the config file, so
  a checked-in cluster config means the same thing wherever it is run from.

EXAMPLES
  # Start the dashboard and open it (Ctrl-C to stop)
  enola dashboard --open

  # Start MCP server with default config
  enola

  # Start MCP server with custom config
  enola my-config.yaml

  # Generate a snapshot and exit
  enola --generate

  # Index a whole cluster from one config (see CONFIG_PATH)
  enola --generate cluster.yaml

  # Re-read one repository of that cluster into the existing union
  enola --generate --refresh ../service-a cluster.yaml

  # Print a statistics report for a repository and exit
  enola --explain /path/to/repo

  # Report over a whole cluster
  enola --explain cluster.yaml

  # Generate snapshot with custom config
  enola --generate my-config.yaml

  # Freeze another repo's architecture before editing it (see THE GATE)
  enola baseline pin /path/to/repo

  # Report what your changes did to it (reports everything, fails nothing)
  enola check /path/to/repo

  # Report what the pinned baseline describes
  enola baseline show /path/to/repo

  # Tell your agents enola is here (instructions only)
  enola install

  # …and close the loop automatically at the end of a session
  enola install --hooks

  # Preview what install would change, writing nothing
  enola install --hooks --dry-run

  # Remove it all again
  enola uninstall

  # Fail on a violation of a layer order you declared
  enola check --fail-on=layers

  # Enforce a policy you set, but only warn this time
  enola check --fail-on=layers --warn-only

  # See which cross-repo edges resolved, and which did not
  enola coverage cluster.yaml

  # Just the blind spots
  enola coverage --unresolved cluster.yaml

  # Are the session hooks actually firing?
  enola doctor

  # Compare against the preceding snapshot instead of the pin
  enola check --baseline=previous

  # Fail on a layer order enola only INFERRED (never reaches 1.00)
  enola check --fail-on=layers --min-confidence=0.8

  # Opt into the cycle check as well
  enola check --fail-on=layers,cycles

  # Did the change stay where you said it would?
  enola check --target=internal/auth

  # …and fail if it reached anywhere else
  enola check --target=internal/auth --max-spillover=0

  # Check MCP server status
  enola --status

  # Show usage broken down per repo
  enola --status --all

  # Check version
  enola --version

DASHBOARD
  Run "enola dashboard [repo_path|config_path]" to serve the latest snapshot
  without starting an MCP server; add --open to launch the browser. It stays attached
  to the terminal until Ctrl-C. The normal MCP
  server also exposes the same read-only dashboard. Data refresh is explicit, so an
  investigation is never interrupted. Run "--status" to find its URL, or pass
  "--no-dashboard" to disable it.

  In an interactive terminal, "--generate", "--refresh", and "check --write"
  each print an "Explore this snapshot" command using "dashboard --open". CI,
  redirected output, hooks, and ENOLA_NO_PROMPTS=1 suppress the hint.

THE GATE — grading a change
  Tests tell you whether behaviour still works. This tells you whether a change
  altered the STRUCTURE of the system in a way nobody asked for — a layer crossed the
  wrong way, coupling between modules that had no business knowing about each other.

  It needs a "before" to compare against, so the order matters:

    1.  enola baseline pin /path/to/repo     # freeze how it looks NOW, before editing
    2.  …make your changes…
    3.  enola check /path/to/repo            # grade what they did

  Step 1 snapshots the repository and freezes it — no separate --generate needed.
  Omit the path to act on the current directory.

  The baseline lives in that repository's own output dir (.enola/baseline), so each
  repo keeps its own and you can hold baselines for several at once. "baseline show"
  reports what the current one describes; "baseline clear" removes it.

  Step 3 is read-only: it writes nothing and leaves the baseline in place, so it can
  be run as often as you like and re-run after a fix. It exits 0 when clean, 1 on a
  structural regression, 2 when it could not run, and 3 when it declined to grade
  because the baseline is not comparable — 3 is never a statement about your change.

  Step 3 fails nothing on its own. It reports every finding the change introduced and
  exits 0; "--fail-on=<explainer,…>" names the ones that should break the build, and
  "--max-spillover=N" bounds how far outside "--target" the change may reach. A run
  with neither says so in its output rather than printing a bare PASS.

  A path that names a DIRECTORY is a repository; one that names a FILE is a config.

UPDATES
  enola checks at most once every 12 hours, in the background, whether a newer
  release exists, and caches the answer in ~/.enola/update.json. No command ever
  waits on the network for it: the notice you see is a read of that file. When
  a newer release is found, "enola doctor" reports it and "enola upgrade"
  installs it.

  The notice says whether the EXTRACTORS changed, not what else did. That single
  bit is the one that matters for your data: it means snapshots from your build
  are missing facts a current enola would extract.

  It is silent for a dev build, and disabled entirely by:

    export ENOLA_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1

  It also never runs when CI is set.

MCP CONFIGURATION
  Add to your MCP client's config (e.g., Cursor mcp.json):

    {
      "mcpServers": {
        "enola": {
          "command": "enola",
          "args": ["mcp-arch.yaml"]
        }
      }
    }

  Or if installed globally via "go install":

    {
      "mcpServers": {
        "enola": {
          "command": "enola"
        }
      }
    }

BUILD
  Version can be set at build time with ldflags:

    go build -ldflags "-X github.com/enola-labs/enola/internal/version.Version=0.1.0" -o enola ./cmd/enola
